WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.923
- Hello!

00:00:00.923 --> 00:00:03.390
I feel like I've had your
family here at this point

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.560
on this show.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.610
I've had a couple of your kids.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.740
I'm just sayin', I've had
a lot of your family here.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.600
- Well, one time, we should
bring us all together here.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.748
- I know, (laughing) that's-

00:00:10.748 --> 00:00:11.581
- Exactly, yeah.

00:00:11.581 --> 00:00:12.414
- Will it be more exciting?

00:00:12.414 --> 00:00:13.247
Are y'all fun at Thanksgiving? (laughing)

00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:14.080
- It would be great, yes.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:14.913
Absolutely, yeah.

00:00:14.913 --> 00:00:16.075
- Awesome.

00:00:16.075 --> 00:00:16.908
- It's great to have Paul.

00:00:16.908 --> 00:00:18.540
- I know, y'all have
met, obviously, right?

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.513
- Yeah, yeah, of course.

00:00:19.513 --> 00:00:20.346
I mean,

00:00:20.346 --> 00:00:23.130
he has played several times
when I did the "Letterman" show.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.037
- Exactly, yes.

00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:26.070
- [Arnold Schwarzenegger] And we always-

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.170
- We have got a funny
connection though, you and I.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.730
I don't know if you realize this,

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:34.320
but before you were, you
know, a great politician,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.680
you made an album, a vinyl album,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.100
called "Arnold Schwarzenegger's
Total Body Workout."

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.800
You working out to the hits of the 80's,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.147
and you worked out to one of my songs,

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:49.950
"It's Raining Men," that I
co-wrote with Paul Jabara.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.385
You have, "One, two,
up, down, up, down, up."

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:55.770
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:56.730
I'm telling you,

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.910
I wanna go to the studio
with you too I think.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.160
- Oh my God, you were like
the original playlist guy,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:02.993
making playlists for workouts.

00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:05.850
- But I mean, that's why that
record was such a big hit.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:06.708
- [Paul Shaffer] I'm sure it was.

00:01:06.708 --> 00:01:08.040
- It was not because of
me going up and down,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.290
and doing my squats, and my leg lifts,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.644
and all of those things,

00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:13.050
but it was because of your music.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.806
- Well, you were no fool.

00:01:14.806 --> 00:01:15.639
- [Arnold Schwarzenegger] I mean-

00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:16.586
- [Kelly Clarkson] Ugh, "It's
Raining Men" is a great song.

00:01:16.586 --> 00:01:18.330
- I know, exactly, I have good
taste when it comes to music.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.424
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:19.424 --> 00:01:20.325
(everyone laughing)

00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:21.158
- [Paul Shaffer] We all know that.

00:01:21.158 --> 00:01:21.991
- You must love mine.

00:01:21.991 --> 00:01:23.055
Okay... Um, I'm just
kidding, I'm just kidding.

00:01:23.055 --> 00:01:25.590
(Paul laughing)
(audience applauding)

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.520
Welcome to our New York studio.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.353
- What do you think why I'm here?

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.474
- Exactly, you're like, "I'm
only coming for the music."

00:01:28.474 --> 00:01:30.180
- I love your music too, exactly, yeah.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.260
- No, welcome to our studio.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.720
This is our first
season, here in New York.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.112
We're very excited, and we're
very excited you're here.

00:01:35.112 --> 00:01:35.945
- [Arnold Schwarzenegger] I know,

00:01:35.945 --> 00:01:36.778
and I'm the first guest, yeah.

00:01:36.778 --> 00:01:38.280
(audience cheering)

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.030
- Do you remember, like when
you first moved over here,

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.230
like do you have a first
memory of New York?

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.770
- I remember coming here in 1968

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.100
and traveling through New York,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.120
then going to Los Angeles
and living on Muscle Beach.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:57.953
- Yeah.

00:01:57.953 --> 00:01:59.700
- But then I came back in 1969,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.730
and then won the Mr. Universe
contest for the fourth time-

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.530
- Like you do, yeah, yeah.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.363
- In New York.

00:02:05.363 --> 00:02:08.340
And so I remember that,
and then right after that,

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.380
I was offered to do
this movie in New York.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.213
- Yeah.

00:02:11.213 --> 00:02:12.840
- That was a really great beginning,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.190
I finally realized my dream.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.330
And then afterwards,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.840
I chased my dream and continued
taking acting lessons.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.690
And then, my movie career took off,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.880
and it was one of those
kind of lucky things.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.290
- When you first moved here though,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.390
you obviously have a thick accent still...

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.490
I do too, welcome to Texas.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:30.450
But-

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.283
- I have an accent?

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:32.820
- What?
(audience laughing)

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.200
So weird.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.150
No, I was just gonna
say, when you moved here,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.010
was there a lot of like
lost in translation

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.270
when you're having
conversations with people?

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.740
Like, you thinking you're saying something

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.270
and they hear something else?

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.120
- Yeah, it's very difficult
to adopt to a new language,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.200
you know?

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.200
I remember that right in the
beginning, a friend of mine,

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.550
a Jewish friend of mine said
that, "I know Austrian food.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.630
There's this Hungarian
restaurant on Fairfax Avenue,"

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.130
he says, "I'm gonna take you there."

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:07.130
So he took us there and I ate
this unbelievable, you know,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.650
vegetables and meats and
everything like that.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.680
And then I said to the guy, to the owner,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.587
he came to the table, he says,

00:03:14.587 --> 00:03:16.560
"Can I help you, do you want some more?"

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.677
And I said, "Yeah, can I have
some more of your garbage?"

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:21.710
- Your garbage?

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:22.543
- [Arnold Schwarzenegger] Yes, and so-

00:03:22.543 --> 00:03:23.376
- So, what were you trying to say?

00:03:23.376 --> 00:03:24.209
- And so my friend...

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:25.463
So he says, "What do you call my food?

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:29.460
Say this again, what do you call my food?

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.450
You called it garbage?"

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.747
And then my friend jumped in and says,

00:03:31.747 --> 00:03:34.350
"No, no, no, what he
actually meant was cabbage.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.508
He wants to have some more
of your cabbage." (laughing)

00:03:37.508 --> 00:03:39.224
I mean, things like that will come up.

00:03:39.224 --> 00:03:40.458
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:03:40.458 --> 00:03:42.725
It was like crazy, yeah. (laughing)

00:03:42.725 --> 00:03:44.880
But I mean, he understand
after, "Oh, cabbage,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.176
you want more of the cabbage."

00:03:46.176 --> 00:03:47.009
I said, "Yeah, yeah, cabbage.

00:03:47.009 --> 00:03:47.842
I'm sorry."

00:03:47.842 --> 00:03:48.990
- Pretty bold of the guy though.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.400
Like, you looking like you,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.967
and like him being bold enough to be like,

00:03:51.967 --> 00:03:52.860
"What did you say?"

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:53.693
I don't know if I'd...

00:03:53.693 --> 00:03:55.800
I'd have just been,
"Exactly, it's garbage, bye."

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.240
I would've just left.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.920
I heard this crazy story, it's random,

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.060
and I have to know if it's true.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.390
Did you accidentally break
a first lady's ankle,

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.290
and how does that happen if so?

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.230
- Well, George Bush invited me...

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:11.514
When I was

00:04:11.514 --> 00:04:13.350
the Chairman of the
President's Council of Fitness,

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.300
he would invite me always to Camp David.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.770
This was 1991/92, when he was president.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:23.130
And he would invite me up there,
literally like every month.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.920
He loved hanging out with
me and working out with me.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.920
We'd do throwing horseshoe, and you know,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.900
going running up there and all this stuff.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.200
And I was one time up there in the winter.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.290
And so he said to me, he says,
"I have this great sled,"

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.690
which was not really a
sled, it was a toboggan.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:40.587
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:40.587 --> 00:04:45.587
- And so with sleds, you can
steer the sled with your feet,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.370
but that one I couldn't really steer.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.400
So we go down the hill in the snow there,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.040
he's sitting behind me
and he says, "You steer,"

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.903
and I did not know how
to steer. (laughing)

00:04:55.903 --> 00:04:58.170
So I mean, it's just
going faster and faster,

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.760
and all of a sudden we're
approaching this tree.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.310
And all of a sudden, Barbara
comes up behind the tree

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.773
and then we just ran into her leg.

00:05:06.624 --> 00:05:09.240
And so the poor Barbara Bush, you know,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.675
she had to go to the hospital afterwards.

00:05:10.675 --> 00:05:11.552
- [Kelly Clarkson] Oh my God.

00:05:11.552 --> 00:05:12.750
- She had, you know, a sprained leg

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.360
and she got a cast on it and
the whole thing like that.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.680
But I have to say that they
were very gracious about it

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:19.973
in the way they took it.

00:05:19.973 --> 00:05:21.240
(audience laughing)

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.910
And you know, I apologized,

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.530
I said, "I feel really
bad about it," yeah.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.850
- [Kelly Clarkson] Nobody has
this story, that's incredible.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:30.600
- [Arnold Schwarzenegger] She
was a wonderful first lady.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.400
- So we wanna talk about your new book.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.280
So it's called "Be Useful:
Seven Tools for Life."

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.003
Wait, I have it right here.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.160
(Kelly singing)

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.080
How did you come up with seven?

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.130
Like, it feels like...

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.600
Did you know the number was gonna be that,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.430
like how did you come up with that number?

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.400
- The key thing is that
you pick specific things

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.650
that really helped you in life.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:50.483
- [Kelly Clarkson] Mm-hmm.

00:05:50.483 --> 00:05:53.700
- And that make your life
fortunate, as as my life is,

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.640
just extraordinary, and to be successful.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.190
But I think it's important to mention,

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.050
I always say, "I'm not a self-made man."

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.737
Because so many people write stories,

00:06:02.737 --> 00:06:05.670
"Schwarzenegger is an
example of a self-made man,"

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.950
and I always say, "I'm not because,"

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.900
I say, "Everything that I've achieved

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.880
is because of the help of other people."

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:12.713
- Absolutely.

00:06:12.713 --> 00:06:13.546
But, that's a true leader.

00:06:13.546 --> 00:06:16.380
- Some American brought me
over to America, Joe Weider,

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.330
I remember he brought me to America.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.620
There were the training partners

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.250
that helped me all the time to
train and to become champion.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:24.120
There were bodybuilders

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.710
that brought over dishes to my
apartment, blankets, pillows,

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.080
a black and white TV, a radio.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.730
I mean, all this stuff.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.070
I mean, you have no
idea what it feels like

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:38.640
when you are this
immigrant, this foreigner

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.790
that has $20 in his pocket
when I came over here.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.251
And then all of a sudden,
people show this generosity-

00:06:44.251 --> 00:06:45.166
- It's love, yeah, kindness.

00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:45.999
- And help you like that.

00:06:45.999 --> 00:06:48.059
And there's love and inclusion.

00:06:48.059 --> 00:06:52.260
It's important to recognize
that you're not a self-made man

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:57.260
because then you realize
how many people helped you,

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:00.990
and then you realize how many
people you have to help also.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:01.955
- Yeah.

00:07:01.955 --> 00:07:02.788
- Because if they helped you,

00:07:02.788 --> 00:07:04.397
now you have to give something back.

00:07:04.397 --> 00:07:05.230
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:06.063
- And I have to help other people.

00:07:06.063 --> 00:07:06.896
That's why in the book,

00:07:06.896 --> 00:07:09.060
the last chapter is "Break your mirror."

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.220
You know, the mirror that makes
you always look at yourself-

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.053
- Yeah.

00:07:12.053 --> 00:07:14.070
- Break that mirror and
just look beyond that,

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:17.310
and you will see the millions
of people that need your help.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:18.930
That's why in the in the 70's,

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.090
I got involved with Special Olympics

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.520
and I started becoming
the national trainer

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.290
and then the international trainer.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.730
And then later on, I became

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.160
the Chairman of the
President's Council of Fitness

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.870
under President Bush.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.180
And then later on, I
started afterschool programs

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:38.180
because I realized that 70%
of the kids come from families

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.290
where both of their families are working.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.500
- Yeah, both the parents, yeah, yeah.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.720
- So both of the parents are working.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.810
So now, there's no one there
for the kids in the afternoon.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:46.710
- Yeah.
- So, what happens?

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.360
- Or there's just one
parent in the household

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:49.830
so they have to have
somewhere to go, yeah.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:51.540
- It's a major, major challenge.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.520
Programs are very important
because it gives you,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.290
in the afternoon, we provide
tutoring for the kids and-

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.123
- That's amazing.

00:07:59.123 --> 00:08:01.740
- Homework assistance, sports programs,

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.457
arts programs, and all that.

00:08:03.457 --> 00:08:04.290
- Yeah.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.300
- And just the key thing is
to have adult supervision.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.130
So they are between three and six o'clock,

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.320
having a great time, studying,

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:11.932
getting better in different areas-

00:08:11.932 --> 00:08:13.176
- Learning teamwork,
learning all this, yeah.

00:08:13.176 --> 00:08:14.009
- Exactly.

00:08:14.009 --> 00:08:15.420
And their education improve tremendously

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.070
and their grades improve tremendously.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:18.265
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:08:18.265 --> 00:08:19.740
- So, it's really a win-win situation.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.260
And the parents can work
without having the headache.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:23.742
So this is just one of the things-

00:08:23.742 --> 00:08:24.575
- 'Cause of worry.

00:08:24.575 --> 00:08:26.910
- That I do to give back to the community.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.160
And then of course,
when I ran for governor,

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.250
I turned my back on
doing movies every year-

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.083
- Yeah.

00:08:33.083 --> 00:08:34.710
- And making this
$20,000,000 a movie a year.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:35.543
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:08:35.543 --> 00:08:36.457
- And then just said,

00:08:36.457 --> 00:08:37.950
"Okay, I'm gonna go and
just be a public servant,

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.192
serve the people of California."

00:08:40.192 --> 00:08:41.127
- But when you're
blessed, you bless others.

00:08:41.127 --> 00:08:42.352
- So, it's all about giving back.

00:08:42.352 --> 00:08:43.185
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:43.185 --> 00:08:44.550
I agree with that, I agree with that.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:45.383
Well I wanna come back

00:08:45.383 --> 00:08:46.216
to the one you were talking about earlier,

00:08:46.216 --> 00:08:47.049
"Breaking Mirrors."

00:08:47.049 --> 00:08:49.500
So it's open your mind
and break mirrors, right?

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.187
What exactly does that... I love that.

00:08:51.187 --> 00:08:54.480
- "Opening Your Mind" is
another chapter in there,

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.970
which is "Open Your Mind
and Shut Your Mouth."

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:57.803
- Yeah.

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:58.872
Oh. (laughing)

00:08:58.872 --> 00:08:59.705
- [Arnold Schwarzenegger] Yeah, because-

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:00.538
- You know what?

00:09:00.538 --> 00:09:01.920
I could use that one. (laughing)

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.120
- No, no, but I'm telling you

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.940
because I wanted to just let people know

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.760
that one of the secrets of my success

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.820
was that I always was like a sponge.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.850
I listened very carefully
what people have to say.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.830
I am a very good listener,

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.290
and I think it really
helped me in my career.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:20.550
It helped me in my acting career,

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:22.530
it helped me in my political career

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.590
that I was more interested in
listening rather than talking.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.080
And so I think it's
just one of those things

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:31.290
because so many people are
so concerned about, you know,

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.310
what they have to say.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.417
But you don't learn
anything if you say it-

00:09:34.417 --> 00:09:35.442
- If your mouth is moving.

00:09:35.442 --> 00:09:37.200
- And you learn when you listen.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.541
You know, God gave us two
ears and only one mouth.

00:09:40.541 --> 00:09:42.000
(Kelly laughing)

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.670
- So, we might as well
just listen twice as much

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.044
and talk less.

00:09:46.044 --> 00:09:46.920
I mean, that's what the
chapter's all about.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.818
- I feel like God sent
you here today for me.

00:09:49.818 --> 00:09:52.440
(everyone laughing)

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:53.940
I feel like this is an intervention,

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.055
and my crew is like, "Yeah, listen."

00:09:56.055 --> 00:09:57.150
(audience laughing)

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:58.080
Well, let's do another break.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:59.130
This is incredible.

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.480
Everybody, y'all gotta go get this.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:01.313
Arnold's new book

00:10:01.313 --> 00:10:04.026
is called "Be Useful:
Seven Tools for Life."

00:10:04.026 --> 00:10:05.198
It's out now,

00:10:05.198 --> 00:10:07.544
and everyone in the
audience is getting a copy!

00:10:07.544 --> 00:10:09.827
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:10:09.827 --> 00:10:12.548
You can also find Arnold's
companies that are online.

